---------------10.4---------------------
Exercise 10.4 Despite many superficial syntactical
similarities between JavaStream and RxJava, the two concepts
are fundamentally different. This exercise focus on some of these differences.
Consider the pseudo-code below (that does not compile and run).
The source() provides english words and
the sink() absorbs them. Note that sink() is a
pseudo-operation that just absorbs the data it receives. Your
explanations should focus on what happens
in between the source() and sink().

1. Describe what happens when this code runs:
source().filter(w -> w.length() > 5).sink()
• as a JavaStream (e.g. the source is a file)
• as a RxJava statement where the source could
  be an input field where a user types strings

--JavaStream:
source(): returns a lazy stream of words,
          that is only used when demanded (by a terminal operation)
filter(w -> w.length() > 5): Intermediate operation that filters out words
                             with length less than 5
sink(): terminal operation that consumes the stream. This means, that filter
        above, doesn't actually start filtering until sink is called

in short:
The process runs on demand(lazily) once and stops after processing all
the elements in the source

--RxJava:

source(): Is an observable that emits words continuously in real time, even
          if there are no subscribers

filter(w -> w.length() > 5): whenever the observable emits a new item that
                             passes the filter, it is pushed to the next operator
sink(): Most likely pushes to a subscriber that consumes the data

in short:
The process runs continuously emitting filtered items to subscribers.


2. Describe what happens when this code runs:
source().filter(w -> w.length() > 5).sink();
source().filter(w -> w.length() > 10).sink()
• as a JavaStream (e.g. the source is a file)
• as a RxJava statement where the source could be
  an input field where a user types strings


--JavaStream:
Each line is independent of the other.
The source will be read twice, once for each operation.
The first statement will happen before the second statement.

--RxJava:
The source() might be the same for both statements.
(same Observable object)
Each pipeline processes emissions independently
and simultaneously as they occur and is continuous.
Both pipelines can handle new
emissions concurrently allowing for real-time processing
of data streams where different criteria can be met by the
same or different items emitted.
